A||||4||Spektyr||EGR question||||5||
Z||000000||Spektyr||04-29-2001||12:44 AM||||Okay, first off I'm lucky enough to live in a state with no emissions testing.  (Kansas)<P>I've heard some people talk about using a block off plate to eliminate the EGR valve and getting more performance.<P>First off, how much difference does that make, and second, what if any bad effects can this have?<P>It seems to me that not rebreathing exhaust gas is an obvious performance gain, and I've already replaced the cat with a straight pipe so I don't have to worry about clogging it with the higher emissions.  Is there anything I'm not thinking of?  Does the ECM get screwy without the EGR installed?<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||04-29-2001||02:58 AM||||Assuming the EGR is working and there are no leaks and the fact that the EGR is closed during full throttle, you won't see any difference when you hold it to the floor. <P>With it blocked you might see:<P>1. That the throttle response to a slam is slightly (and slightly) better since there is a slight delay in closing.<P>2. At light cruise you very well might get pinging since the ECM increases the advance to compensate for the EGR gases. Without it, you are too advanced.<P>3. The NOX will go up.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||04-29-2001||03:24 AM||||In order to reduce pinging, You'll need to use the premium grade gas 92/93 octane and advance your timing a little. Lower octane and no EGR will raise head temps (cause pinging/pre-dentonation knock). With the price of gas going up this summer, Do you want to always pay the premium price?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spektyr||04-29-2001||07:03 AM||||Hmm... looks like EGR = good.  I think I'll leave mine on for the time being.  hehe||||reg||1
Z||000004||SteveJ||04-29-2001||07:54 AM||||When egr valves first came in lots of people wanted to block 'em off. Seems I remember that the engineers designing these engines counted on the cooling effect of the exaust gas to reduce emissions but also designed the valves in the head to operate in that environment. The caution at the time was burned valves.||||reg||1
